Subject: [PATCH] tb710fu: persist COM5 CDC across all kernel panics and halts

Every panic on this board terminates on the panicking CPU inside
panic_spin_forever() or panic_spin_shmcon(), never in halt_all_cpus():

  * boot-args debug=0x14e contains DB_NMI (0x4), and on arm64 debug.c:449
    turns DB_NMI into panicDebugging = TRUE, so debugger_collect_diagnostics()
    takes the `PE_i_can_has_debugger(NULL) && panicDebugging` branch (line
    1678), skips `if (!panicDebugging) kdp_machine_reboot_type(...)` (line
    1705) and ends in panic_spin_forever() (line 1710);
  * panic_spin_shmcon() is the branch before that, and this device has no
    astris/hardware shared-memory debugger, so its `for (;;) { wfe }` has
    nobody to talk to.

Two independent failures followed from that, both seen in pl44 (run
45553701071, COM5 log com5_pl44_gop.log):

  1. patch 0045's tb710fu_cdc_panic_loop() hook lives in halt_all_cpus(), so
     it is unreachable in this configuration and the panic loop never ran.
     The commit claim "enter tb710fu_cdc_panic_loop() before kPEPanicRestartCPU"
     was not implemented by this patch; only the nested-panic assert was.
  2. patch 0004 puts tb710fu_ramoops_panic_reset() first in
     panic_spin_forever() and in kdp_machine_reboot_type(); that flushes, waits
     ramoops_panic_delay (default 15 s) and issues PSCI SYSTEM_RESET. The
     console bytes of the panic were already tapped into the 64 KiB CDC ring
     (patch 0010 _cnputc/PE_kputc tap) but the ring is only transmitted by the
     DWC3 poll loop, so the reset ended the boot first: COM5 saw no panic
     byte at all and the port was gone afterwards (reopen returned 31).

This patch hands the controller to the CDC shell at every terminal point,
before anything can reset the SoC or park the CPU:

  * debugger_collect_diagnostics(): immediately after SavePanicInfo() has
    written the panic log, and before on_device_corefile_enabled() can start
    a disk coredump. Drain 6 s, flush the ramoops zone and enter
    tb710fu_cdc_panic_loop(), which keeps polling DWC3 with interrupts off and
    executes the hands-free 'reboot' / 'edl' / 'help' verbs. A boot that never
    adopted a controller returns 0 here and every upstream path is unchanged.
  * panic_spin_shmcon(): the hand-over is the first statement, before the
    PE_i_can_has_debugger()/hwsd_info checks that return on this board before
    the WFE loop, so the astris spin can no longer park the CPU on a board
    without astris.
  * panic_spin_forever(): the same hand-over at the top, before the ramoops
    reset, so a path that reaches it without diagnostics still delivers.
  * kdp_machine_reboot_type(): drain 4 s before tb710fu_ramoops_panic_reset(),
    so a reset that is going to happen cannot preempt the log either.
  * Remove the nested assert(panic_info) in PE_update_panicheader_nestedpanic()
    when gPanicBase == 0: there is no iBoot panic VRAM region on TB710FU, and
    the assert turned a nested panic into limit 5 and bypassed every hook.
  * model_dep.c SavePanicInfo(): return before the embedded-panic-header
    bookkeeping when panic_info is NULL. pl45b (run 45563220726) showed the
    nested panic this replaces: the watchdogd[262] critical-exit panic was
    followed by "Kernel data abort ... pc 0xfffffff00bba2124, lr
    0xfffffff00b976694, far 0x000000000000000c". Note that the "kernel text
    exec slide/base" pair printed by the nested-panic helper does not reconcile
    with the shipped image (0x1b00000 of the quoted 0x4920000 is the
    KC-header-to-__TEXT_EXEC delta, not a relocation), so the pc/lr are used
    exactly as printed: disassembling out/artifacts/tb710fu-pl45b-45563220726/
    kernel/kernel.macho (__TEXT_EXEC, file offset 0x4b9e124) resolves
    SavePanicInfo()'s panic-option arms:
      0xfffffff00bba2100  ldr   x8, [x8, #0x5a8]  ; panic_info, __DATA
      0xfffffff00bba2114  tbnz  w19, #0         ; PANICLOGANDREBOOT   -> 0x1
      0xfffffff00bba2118  tbnz  w19, #6         ; COMPANION_PROC      -> 0x40
      0xfffffff00bba211c  tbnz  w19, #0xe       ; INTEGRATED_COPROC   -> 0x4000
      0xfffffff00bba2120  tbz   w19, #0xd, ...  ; USERSPACE_INITIATED (0x2000)
      0xfffffff00bba2124  ldur  x9, [x8, #0xc]  ; <-- fault, far = 0xc
      0xfffffff00bba2128  orr   x9, x9, #0x20000
      0xfffffff00bba212c  stur  x9, [x8, #0xc]  ; eph_panic_flags |= 0x20000
    i.e. panic_info->eph_panic_flags at NULL + 0xc, with panic_info NULL because
    gPanicBase is 0 on this board ("debug_log_init: Error!! gPanicBase is still
    not initialized"). The abort replaced the real panic string with the nested
    one and never returned to panic_spin_forever(), so the drain/loop added
    here could not run.

Source: pristine XNU ac9718fb1af618d5ce8678d0dc6e8a58f252216f with the
selected UniXNU arch/SoC/macOS series and patches 0001..0048 applied in series
order; the pre-change source SHA-256 of each edited file is in that patch's own
header (0004 owns the debug.c panic_spin_forever()/kdp_machine_reboot_type()
hunk, 0010 owns the CDC tap, 0045 owns the halt_all_cpus() hook). pe_init.c,
model_dep.c and out_shmem.c are otherwise untouched by this series, debug.c is
touched by 0004 only.

Reason: a debug boot must deliver its own panic text over the channel that is
already open (COM5, dtr=1) and must keep the shell that can reset the device
hands-free. A reset before the drain destroys both, and so does a data abort
inside the panic path: it must not be possible to nest a panic on this board.

The drain provider is package/Tb710fuUsbCdc/CdcXnu.c. It now proves ownership
with the controller's adopted flag, set only at the end of a completed
tb710fu_dwc3_start(), instead of probe_trace[1]: that value is the DEVTEN
readback taken before RUN_STOP is raised and can be zero on an initialized
controller, which skipped the panic loop exactly when it was needed. On
hand-over tb710fu_dwc3_panic_rearm() is called unconditionally and reclaims
every endpoint (EP0 SETUP, Bulk RX, Bulk TX) a halted worker may have left
owned; pump() retires a Bulk transfer still owned after BULK_RETIRE_POLLS, so a
frozen worker CPU can never deadlock COM5 in panic mode. The loop appends
"TB710FU CDC: panic loop active (type edl/reboot)" to the CDC ring and executes
the safe verbs (edl/reboot/bootloader/recovery/help).

Verification: the debug.c and out_shmem.c hunks are generated from, and
re-apply to, the exact ac9718f sources carrying patch 0004's debug.c changes;
the other files' hunks are unchanged from the previously applied revision of
this patch. git apply --stat parses the whole file. This is a source and
patch-application check; hardware panic drain, COM5 reopen and hands-free
'reboot' still require a native run.

diff --git a/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c b/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c
index fd449f7..20377d9 100644
--- a/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c
+++ b/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c
@@ -737,8 +737,7 @@ PE_update_panicheader_nestedpanic()
 	 * if the panic header pointer is bogus (e.g. someone stomped on it) then bail.
 	 */
 	if (!panic_info) {
-		/* if this happens in development then blow up bigly */
-		assert(panic_info);
+		/* No iBoot panic VRAM region on TB710FU; avoid nested panic so the CDC panic loop stays reachable. */
 		return;
 	}
 
diff --git a/osfmk/kern/debug.c b/osfmk/kern/debug.c
--- a/osfmk/kern/debug.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/debug.c
@@ -1371,6 +1371,26 @@ panic_trap_to_debugger(const char *panic_format_str, va_list *panic_args, unsign
 void __attribute__((noinline, optnone))
 panic_spin_forever(void)
 {
+#if defined(XNU_TB710FU_CDC) && XNU_TB710FU_CDC
+	/*
+	 * Every panic on this board terminates on this CPU: debug=0x14e sets DB_NMI,
+	 * which sets panicDebugging, so halt_all_cpus() - and its CDC hook - never
+	 * runs. Reclaim every endpoint a halted worker may have left owned, drain
+	 * the bytes already tapped and keep the shell: COM5 holds the panic log and
+	 * the hands-free 'reboot' / 'edl' commands still execute. Without an adopted
+	 * controller this returns 0 and the ramoops reset below is unchanged.
+	 */
+	{
+		extern int  tb710fu_cdc_panic_drain(unsigned milliseconds);
+		extern void tb710fu_ramoops_flush(void);
+		extern void tb710fu_cdc_panic_loop(void);
+
+		if (tb710fu_cdc_panic_drain(6000u)) {
+			tb710fu_ramoops_flush();
+			tb710fu_cdc_panic_loop();
+		}
+	}
+#endif
 	/* TB710FU: flush the zone and reset, so the panic survives to Android. */
 	tb710fu_ramoops_panic_reset();
 	for (;;) {
@@ -1393,6 +1413,17 @@ panic_stackshot_release_lock(void)
 static void
 kdp_machine_reboot_type(unsigned int type, uint64_t debugger_flags)
 {
+#if defined(XNU_TB710FU_CDC) && XNU_TB710FU_CDC
+	/*
+	 * The reset below is what makes COM5 disappear. Give the bytes already
+	 * sitting in the CDC ring to the host first.
+	 */
+	{
+		extern int tb710fu_cdc_panic_drain(unsigned milliseconds);
+
+		(void)tb710fu_cdc_panic_drain(4000u);
+	}
+#endif
 	if (type == kPEPanicRestartCPU) {
 		tb710fu_ramoops_panic_reset();
 	}
@@ -1550,6 +1581,27 @@ debugger_collect_diagnostics(unsigned int exception, unsigned int code, unsigned
 		}
 	}
 
+#if defined(XNU_TB710FU_CDC) && XNU_TB710FU_CDC
+	/*
+	 * The panic log and stackshot are already written (SavePanicInfo above).
+	 * On this board there is no local corefile to protect and every path below
+	 * only parks the CPU or resets the SoC: hand the controller to the CDC shell
+	 * here, before on_device_corefile_enabled() can start a disk coredump and
+	 * before panic_spin_shmcon() can park on WFE. The panic text then drains
+	 * over COM5 and 'edl' / 'reboot' stay live.
+	 */
+	{
+		extern int  tb710fu_cdc_panic_drain(unsigned milliseconds);
+		extern void tb710fu_ramoops_flush(void);
+		extern void tb710fu_cdc_panic_loop(void);
+
+		if (tb710fu_cdc_panic_drain(6000u)) {
+			tb710fu_ramoops_flush();
+			tb710fu_cdc_panic_loop();
+		}
+	}
+#endif
+
 #if CONFIG_KDP_INTERACTIVE_DEBUGGING
 	/*
 	 * If reboot on panic is enabled and the caller of panic indicated that we should skip
diff --git a/osfmk/kdp/output_stages/out_shmem.c b/osfmk/kdp/output_stages/out_shmem.c
--- a/osfmk/kdp/output_stages/out_shmem.c
+++ b/osfmk/kdp/output_stages/out_shmem.c
@@ -650,6 +650,26 @@ shmem_unmark_as_busy(void)
 void
 panic_spin_shmcon(void)
 {
+#if defined(XNU_TB710FU_CDC) && XNU_TB710FU_CDC
+	/*
+	 * This device has no astris and no hardware shared-memory debugger:
+	 * hwsd_info is NULL, so the checks below return before the WFE loop and the
+	 * panicking CPU would park with nobody to talk to. Hand the CPU to the CDC
+	 * shell first. A boot that adopted no controller falls through and keeps
+	 * the handshake below untouched, as does any boot with a real debugger.
+	 */
+	{
+		extern int  tb710fu_cdc_panic_drain(unsigned milliseconds);
+		extern void tb710fu_ramoops_flush(void);
+		extern void tb710fu_cdc_panic_loop(void);
+
+		if (tb710fu_cdc_panic_drain(6000u)) {
+			tb710fu_ramoops_flush();
+			tb710fu_cdc_panic_loop();
+		}
+	}
+#endif
+
 	if (!PE_i_can_has_debugger(NULL)) {
 		return;
 	}
diff --git a/osfmk/arm/model_dep.c b/osfmk/arm/model_dep.c
index be2252c..ae3a147 100644
--- a/osfmk/arm/model_dep.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm/model_dep.c
@@ -947,7 +947,25 @@ SavePanicInfo(
 	 * thus makes them easier to debug. If you attached to a device
 	 * and see your PC here, look down a few frames to see your
 	 * early-boot panic there.
+	 *
+	 * TB710FU: this board has no iBoot macOS panic region, so gPanicBase and
+	 * gPanicSize are 0 and PE_init_panic_log() leaves panic_info NULL ("debug_log_init:
+	 * Error!! gPanicBase is still not initialized"). The loop below can then never be
+	 * satisfied: on pl44 it spun the panicking CPU forever, and when the header update
+	 * below is reached with panic_info NULL it data-aborts on
+	 * panic_info->eph_panic_flags at NULL + 0xc (pl45b: pc 0xfffffff00bba2124,
+	 * far 0x000000000000000c), which replaces the real panic string with a nested panic
+	 * and never reaches panic_spin_forever() - and with it tb710fu_cdc_panic_drain().
+	 * There is no header to persist here, so skip the save and keep the panic path
+	 * alive on the console, in ramoops and over COM5.
 	 */
+#if defined(XNU_TB710FU_MACOS27_SERVICES) && XNU_TB710FU_MACOS27_SERVICES
+	if (!panic_info) {
+		printf("TB710FU boot-policy: no embedded panic header; panic info save skipped\n");
+		return;
+	}
+#endif
+
 	while (!panic_info || panic_info->eph_panic_log_offset == 0) {
 		// rdar://87170225 (PanicHardening: audit panic code for naked spinloops)
 		// rdar://88094367 (Add test hooks for panic at different stages in XNU)
diff --git a/osfmk/kern/tb710fu_ramoops.c b/osfmk/kern/tb710fu_ramoops.c
index 31048a5..de7e950 100644
--- a/osfmk/kern/tb710fu_ramoops.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/tb710fu_ramoops.c
@@ -452,6 +452,15 @@ tb710fu_ramoops_init(void)
 	}
 }
 
+void
+tb710fu_ramoops_flush(void)
+{
+	if (zone == NULL) {
+		return;
+	}
+	ramoops_zone_flush();
+}
+
 void
 tb710fu_ramoops_panic_reset(void)
 {
diff --git a/osfmk/kern/tb710fu_ramoops.h b/osfmk/kern/tb710fu_ramoops.h
index 3a33840..bd544fa 100644
--- a/osfmk/kern/tb710fu_ramoops.h
+++ b/osfmk/kern/tb710fu_ramoops.h
@@ -4,4 +4,5 @@
 /* Console mirror into the Linux ramoops console zone (boot-arg ramoops=<PA>). */
 void tb710fu_ramoops_init(void);          /* after arm_vm_init */
 void tb710fu_ramoops_putc(char c);        /* every console character */
+void tb710fu_ramoops_flush(void);         /* sync ECC now, no reset */
 void tb710fu_ramoops_panic_reset(void);   /* flush, wait, PSCI SYSTEM_RESET */
